Upstream provenance for the whatsmyname pin in requirements.txt.

requirements.txt can only carry a URL and a hash; this file records where the
pin came from and why it is shaped the way it is.

  Project:   WhatsMyName-Python
  Upstream:  https://github.com/C3n7ral051nt4g3ncy/WhatsMyName-Python
  Commit:    097698240d6df4822a7a475b8989a934e5ebb684
  URL:       https://github.com/C3n7ral051nt4g3ncy/WhatsMyName-Python/archive/097698240d6df4822a7a475b8989a934e5ebb684.tar.gz
  sha256:    c63eb9abd7cf22f18b3b2b466fbe5a82df58d64e1dd3053aea07ff3269d3abf9
  Size:      26165 bytes
  Fetched:   2026-09-17

Why a commit and not a version
------------------------------
Upstream is not published on PyPI and carries no git tags. pyproject.toml
declares version 1.3.0, which has stood unchanged across several commits, so
1.3.0 alone would not identify what we shipped. The Debian version is
1.3.0+git20260429.0976982: upstream's own version, the date of the pinned
commit, and its short hash.

Why an archive URL and not git+https
------------------------------------
pip's --require-hashes cannot hash a VCS URL -- pip refuses a git+https://
requirement in hash-checking mode outright. It can hash an archive. Using the
commit archive keeps whatsmyname and its dependencies (requests, tqdm) inside
one hashed requirements.txt resolved by uv, so the build needs no second fetch
mechanism and no unhashed download.

Why github.com/.../archive/ and not codeload.github.com/.../tar.gz/
-------------------------------------------------------------------
uv rejects a direct URL whose path does not end in a recognised archive
extension, which the codeload form does not. The two were fetched and compared
here: byte-identical, same sha256, 26165 bytes. github.com/.../archive/<sha>
.tar.gz simply redirects to the codeload path.

Refreshing the pin
------------------
Pick the new commit, then regenerate the lock (this replaces requirements.txt
wholesale, header included -- see the header for the exact uv command), verify
the recorded sha256 against a separate fetch of the same URL, and update the
version in debian/changelog and the fields above together.
